"""Abstract (final text, 248 words or fewer; every number was checked against results/revision/*.json)."""
ABSTRACT = 'Meta-Prompted Instruction Refinement (MPIR) asks a language model to score an optimized prompt against a seven-criterion rubric, rewrite it, and keep the rewrite only if it beats the original on validation questions. We re-test it after an earlier evaluation was found to have a validation leak, using a repaired, fully logged loop and decision rules fixed in advance with dated amendments. The scope is narrow: Qwen3-1.7B as target, 45 base prompts from three small-model optimizers on nine Big-Bench Hard tasks, many of them weak. With the 1.7B model refining its own prompts, the original treatment changed the prompt in 1 of 80 runs (exact 95% upper bound 5.8%), but 93-95% of its candidates had lost the answer format, so this test could not tell a failed idea from a failed treatment. A format- and example-preserving re-run (V2F) followed a positive control in which the gate accepted a known-good candidate in 24 of 26 cells. Candidates were then accepted in 15 of 44 runs, but the final prompts won in 1 cell and lost in 3 under a lenient metric validated on 400 AI-labelled responses. In exploratory probes with one run per condition, a 235B refiner won in 4 of 9 cells under strict scoring, mostly format repairs for which a rubric-free rewrite obtains gains of the same kind (paired difference +2.7 points, interval [-3.5, +9.7]); an 8B refiner changed the prompt in 1 of 9 runs. Separately, one answer-format exemplar changed strict accuracy by +19 (APE) and +42 (ProTeGi) points on average, mostly through the tag convention, and identical prompts disagree on 6.5% of examples between scorings. Conclusions are limited to Qwen3 targets, nine tasks and the optimizers tested.'
